# The first language lesson: greetings for arriving and leaving, the three times of day, please,
# thank you and sorry, met in the ferry town's square.
lesson language-hello v=1 format=teach grade=1 unit=1 subject=language levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "[[greet.hello|cap]] and [[greet.goodbye]]"
  goal "Greet someone in [[language.name]], say goodbye, and say please and thank you."

  look {
    scene 35x14 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=start {
        comic hello who=["girl", "boy", "gran"] says=["[[greet.hello-leo]]", "[[greet.hello-ana-good-morning]]", "[[greet.goodbye-thank-you]]"] moods=["happy", "happy", "happy"] with=["", "", ""] thinks=[0, 0, 0] holds=["", "", ""] labels=["1", "2", "3"]
      }
    }
    say """
      In the ferry town people say [[greet.hello]] when they meet and [[greet.goodbye]] when they
      go. In the morning they say [[greet.good-morning]], after lunch [[greet.good-afternoon]], and
      at night [[greet.good-night]]. Please is [[greet.please]] and thank you is
      [[greet.thank-you]]. Say them aloud with a grown-up, who has a line on how each one sounds.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked language.hello-name k=4
    }
    show language.hello-name k=3 easy-k=0 hard-k=6
    show language.hello-meaning k=4 easy-k=5 hard-k=8
    show language.hello-write k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=3
    show language.hello-name k=5 easy-k=2 hard-k=7
    show language.hello-match k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=3
    show language.hello-write k=4 easy-k=3 hard-k=2
    show language.hello-meaning k=1 easy-k=6 hard-k=5
    show language.hello-say k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    level hard {
      show language.hello-write k=1
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show language.hello-why k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show language.hello-puzzle k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "[[greet.hello|cap]] to meet and [[greet.goodbye]] to go. [[greet.please|cap]] is please and [[greet.thank-you]] is thank you."
  }

  grown-ups """
    This is the first lesson in the language. The wrong idea to watch for is that [[greet.hello]]
    and [[greet.goodbye]] are one word for any greeting, and that [[greet.good-morning]] works all
    day; each question can be settled by asking "is she coming or going?" and "what time of day is
    it?". The phrases are on the child's page, so read them aloud together: [[greet.hello]] is said
    [[greet.hello|say]], and [[greet.goodbye]] is said [[greet.goodbye|say]]. The device can say the
    phrases too, but your voice is the way in: sound is a second channel here, never the only one.
    If the child is stuck, act the moments out: walk in and say [[greet.hello]], walk out and say
    [[greet.goodbye]]. Off the page, use one greeting in the language at every meal today. It meets
    England's key stage 2 languages programme (years 3 to 6, statutory from age 7), which asks
    children to "listen attentively to spoken language and show understanding by joining in and
    responding"; Japan's grades 3 and 4 foreign language activities, "Greet, thank and give simple
    instructions and respond to them by using basic expressions"; and the Council of Europe's CEFR
    level A1, "Can understand and use familiar everyday expressions and very basic phrases". The
    phrases are in [[language.variety]].
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level [[greet.please]] is worked first in the teacher's pen, the words to name are [[greet.hello]] and [[greet.thank-you]] and the words to write are [[greet.goodbye]] and [[greet.yes]], the plainer ones of the lesson, with the first set to match and the first version of each star question."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the words to name are [[greet.sorry]] and [[greet.good-night]] and the words to write are [[greet.yes]], [[greet.thank-you]] and [[greet.goodbye]], the ones most often mixed up, with the last set to match, a third word to write and the last version of each star question."
  }
}
